1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,701
This is ECE 320, video 6: the single-cycle datapath, from lecture 4, part 1.

2
00:00:08,151 --> 00:00:28,354
After this video, you can name every part of the datapath. You can build the datapath for eight instructions, one part at a time. You can trace an instruction phase by phase, and say which value flows on each wire. You can build the I, S and B immediates. And you can read a timing diagram.

3
00:00:28,804 --> 00:00:44,740
Before we build, we need three simple parts. The first part is the multiplexer, or mux. It is the trapezoid shape in the datapath drawings. This mux has two inputs, numbered 0 and 1, one output, and a select signal.

4
00:00:45,190 --> 00:00:53,659
The select signal comes from the control. When the select is 0, the mux passes input 0. Here the output is 20.

5
00:00:54,109 --> 00:01:02,003
When the select is 1, the mux passes input 1. Now the output is minus 50. The other input is ignored.

6
00:01:02,453 --> 00:01:10,453
A mux can have more inputs. This one has three, so its select needs two bits. It passes input 0, 1 or 2.

7
00:01:10,903 --> 00:01:25,751
So a mux passes one of its inputs, and the select names the input. A mux has no clock. When the select changes, the output changes. In this video, every time two values want the same wire, we add a mux.

8
00:01:26,201 --> 00:01:38,233
The second part is an adder. The +4 box has one fixed job: its output is its input plus 4. When the PC is 1000, the output is 1004.

9
00:01:38,683 --> 00:01:51,824
The third part is the ALU, the arithmetic logic unit. It has two 32-bit inputs, A and B, and one output. The control signal ALUSel names the operation.

10
00:01:52,274 --> 00:01:58,973
With A equal to 10, B equal to 20, and ALUSel equal to Add, the output is 30.

11
00:01:59,423 --> 00:02:04,009
With ALUSel equal to Sub, the same inputs give minus 10.

12
00:02:04,459 --> 00:02:15,083
ALUSel can also pick and, or, exclusive or, shifts, and set less than. The slides assume that you already know how an ALU computes.

13
00:02:15,533 --> 00:02:26,221
The adder, the ALU, and the muxes are combinational. They have no clock and keep no value. A short time after an input changes, the output changes.

14
00:02:26,671 --> 00:02:39,749
A processor has two parts. The first part is the datapath: the functional units such as the ALU, the registers, and the memory. Data flows through it from the PC to the write back.

15
00:02:40,199 --> 00:02:50,417
The second part is the control. It sets the mux select signals and the write enable signals. They decide where data flows, and which state changes.

16
00:02:50,867 --> 00:03:01,577
The control reads the instruction, and translates the opcode into control signals. This video builds the datapath. Video 7 builds the control.

17
00:03:02,027 --> 00:03:16,064
This processor executes one instruction on every clock tick. The state elements are the PC, the instruction memory, the register file, and the data memory. Their current values drive the logic.

18
00:03:16,514 --> 00:03:23,106
The combinational logic computes with these values. Its outputs settle before the next clock edge.

19
00:03:23,556 --> 00:03:31,023
At the rising clock edge, all state elements store the outputs of the logic. Then the next cycle starts.

20
00:03:31,473 --> 00:03:41,499
So reads are combinational, and a state element changes only at the rising edge. One clock period must be long enough for the whole instruction.

21
00:03:41,949 --> 00:03:54,408
Every instruction runs through up to five phases. This is the full datapath that we build in this video. Phase 1 is fetch. The PC gives the address to the instruction memory.

22
00:03:54,858 --> 00:04:04,415
Phase 2 is decode and register read. The register file reads the source registers, and the immediate generator builds the immediate.

23
00:04:04,865 --> 00:04:10,412
Phase 3 is execute. The ALU computes, and the branch comparator compares.

24
00:04:10,862 --> 00:04:16,025
Phase 4 is memory. A load reads the data memory, and a store writes it.

25
00:04:16,475 --> 00:04:23,323
Phase 5 is register write. In this processor, all five phases happen in one clock cycle.

26
00:04:23,773 --> 00:04:44,317
Now we draw the full datapath that this video builds. It has only about ten parts. We add them one at a time, in the order that an instruction uses them. The new part is orange. First, the PC, the program counter. It is a register. It holds the address of the instruction that runs now.

27
00:04:44,767 --> 00:04:53,513
IMEM is the instruction memory. Its input is the PC. Its output is the 32 bits of the instruction at that address.

28
00:04:53,963 --> 00:05:01,110
The plus 4 adder computes PC plus 4. That is the address of the next instruction in program order.

29
00:05:01,560 --> 00:05:13,848
The instruction bits fan out to several parts. A label such as inst 19 to 15 means bits 19 to 15 of the instruction. These bits hold the rs1 number.

30
00:05:14,298 --> 00:05:31,557
Reg is the register file: the 32 registers x0 to x31. It reads two registers, rs1 and rs2, on DataA and DataB. It writes the value on DataD into register rd, at the clock edge.

31
00:05:32,007 --> 00:05:44,487
Imm Gen is the immediate generator. It takes the immediate bits out of the instruction, and builds a 32-bit number. ImmSel tells it the format: I, S, B or J.

32
00:05:44,937 --> 00:05:53,897
The branch comparator compares the two register values. It tells the control if they are equal, and if the first is less than the second.

33
00:05:54,347 --> 00:06:10,923
The ASel and BSel muxes choose the two ALU inputs. ASel picks register rs1 or the PC. BSel picks register rs2 or the immediate. The ALU computes the operation that ALUSel names.

34
00:06:11,373 --> 00:06:23,149
DMEM is the data memory. Its address comes from the ALU. A load reads data on DataR. A store writes the data on DataW, at the clock edge.

35
00:06:23,599 --> 00:06:35,161
The WBSel mux chooses the value to write into register rd: the memory data, the ALU result, or PC plus 4. This path is the write back.

36
00:06:35,611 --> 00:06:46,534
The PCSel mux chooses the next PC: PC plus 4, or the ALU result. The ALU result is the target of a branch or a jump.

37
00:06:46,984 --> 00:06:59,059
Last, the control, in purple. It reads the instruction, and sets every mux select and every write enable. This video builds the datapath. Video 7 builds the control.

38
00:06:59,509 --> 00:07:11,605
How do we build a processor for an ISA? Look at the ISA, and make sure that the datapath can do every instruction. The slides use these seven instructions, in this order.

39
00:07:12,055 --> 00:07:26,156
Each instruction adds some hardware, shown in orange. add needs the register file and the ALU. sub needs the ALU select signal. addi needs the immediate generator and a mux. And so on.

40
00:07:26,606 --> 00:07:32,985
Most other instructions are similar from the datapath point of view. At the end, we check jal.

41
00:07:33,435 --> 00:07:50,395
The datapath needs elements that keep values between cycles. A register is an array of D flip-flops with a shared clock and a write enable. It stores D at the rising edge, only if the write enable is 1. The PC is a register.

42
00:07:50,845 --> 00:08:03,389
The ISA registers are in a register file, with two read ports and one write port. That is the most that one instruction needs: an R-type reads two registers and writes one.

43
00:08:03,839 --> 00:08:12,351
The data buses are 32 bits wide. The address buses are 5 bits wide, because there are 32 registers.

44
00:08:12,801 --> 00:08:22,635
The memory has one address, one data input for writes, and one data output for reads. It has one port: one read or one write per cycle.

45
00:08:23,085 --> 00:08:29,699
Reads are combinational. Writes happen at the rising edge, only when the write enable is 1.

46
00:08:30,149 --> 00:08:45,018
Every instruction starts with fetch. The PC is a register. It holds the address of the current instruction. It gives this address to the instruction memory, IMEM. IMEM returns the 32 instruction bits.

47
00:08:45,468 --> 00:08:58,375
An adder computes PC plus 4. This is the default next PC. Memory is byte addressed, and one instruction is 4 bytes, so the next instruction is 4 addresses later.

48
00:08:58,825 --> 00:09:08,766
At the rising clock edge, the PC stores PC plus 4. Now the PC is 1004, and IMEM gives the next instruction.

49
00:09:09,216 --> 00:09:21,163
The first instruction is add. Register rd gets register rs1 plus register rs2. Here is add x1, x2, x3 in the R-format.

50
00:09:21,613 --> 00:09:34,391
The rs1 field, bits 19 to 15, goes straight to address port A. Here it is 2. The rs2 field, bits 24 to 20, goes to address port B. Here it is 3.

51
00:09:34,841 --> 00:09:40,217
The rd field, bits 11 to 7, goes to the write address. Here it is 1.

52
00:09:40,667 --> 00:09:50,587
funct7, funct3 and the opcode go to the control. The register numbers sit at the same bits in every format, so they need no decoding.

53
00:09:51,037 --> 00:10:16,744
Now we trace add x1, x2, x3. The bar at the top shows the instruction, its fields, and the given values. It stays on the screen. The PC is 1000, x2 is 10, and x3 is 20. The drawing starts almost empty, and it grows as the instruction moves. In each step, the new part is orange. Phase 1, fetch. The PC holds 1000.

54
00:10:17,194 --> 00:10:29,674
The PC sends 1000 to IMEM, the instruction memory. IMEM outputs the 32 bits at that address. They are the bits in the bar: add x1, x2, x3.

55
00:10:30,124 --> 00:10:39,980
At the same time, the plus 4 adder computes 1000 plus 4, which is 1004. We need this value at the end of the cycle.

56
00:10:40,430 --> 00:10:54,489
Phase 2, decode and register read. The instruction bits fan out. Look at the rs1 field in the bar: bits 19 to 15 hold 2. They go to address port A of the register file.

57
00:10:54,939 --> 00:11:06,117
The register file outputs register 2 on DataA. The bar gives x2 equal to 10. A read is combinational, so it does not wait for a clock edge.

58
00:11:06,567 --> 00:11:16,167
The rs2 field, bits 24 to 20, holds 3. It goes to address port B, and DataB outputs x3, which is 20.

59
00:11:16,617 --> 00:11:30,100
Phase 3, execute. funct7 and funct3 tell the control that this is an add. DataA and DataB go to the ALU, and the ALU adds them. 10 plus 20 is 30.

60
00:11:30,550 --> 00:11:41,942
add does not use memory, so phase 4 does nothing. Phase 5, write back. The wire from the ALU carries 30 back to DataD, the write data port.

61
00:11:42,392 --> 00:11:54,445
The rd field, bits 11 to 7, holds 1. It goes to AddrD. The control sets RegWEn to 1, so the write is enabled. But the register does not change yet.

62
00:11:54,895 --> 00:12:08,549
At the rising clock edge, two state elements change. x1 becomes 30. The PC loads 1004, on the wire from the adder back to the PC. Then the next instruction starts.

63
00:12:08,999 --> 00:12:24,785
Here is the whole datapath for add, as the slides draw it. The values of our example stay on it. Gray parts exist, but add does not use them. The new hardware for add is orange: Reg[], the ALU, their wires, RegWEn.

64
00:12:25,235 --> 00:12:35,667
Slide 18 shows the timing of two add instructions. Time runs to the right. At the first rising edge, the PC becomes 1000.

65
00:12:36,117 --> 00:12:46,869
A short time later, the adder gives 1004, and IMEM gives the instruction. Each value settles a short time after the value that it depends on.

66
00:12:47,319 --> 00:12:51,138
Next, the register file outputs Reg 2 and Reg 3.

67
00:12:51,588 --> 00:12:55,471
Then the ALU output settles at Reg 2 plus Reg 3.

68
00:12:55,921 --> 00:13:07,163
Reg 1 keeps its old value during the whole cycle. It changes only at the next rising edge, because RegWEn is 1. Then the second add starts.

69
00:13:07,613 --> 00:13:22,760
Here is a short check. x1 is 5. The processor runs add x1, x1, x1. Which value of x1 does the ALU read, and what is x1 after the edge? Pause the video and try it.

70
00:13:23,210 --> 00:13:34,111
During the cycle, the register file reads x1 on both ports. The write has not happened yet. So both inputs are 5, and the ALU output is 10.

71
00:13:34,561 --> 00:13:41,665
At the rising edge, x1 becomes 10. A read in the same cycle always sees the old value.

72
00:13:42,115 --> 00:13:53,379
The second instruction is sub. Its meaning is: register rd gets register rs1 minus register rs2. Compare its encoding with add.

73
00:13:53,829 --> 00:14:02,832
Only one bit is different: bit 30. It is 0 for add and 1 for sub. So sub uses exactly the same wires as add.

74
00:14:03,282 --> 00:14:16,018
The only change is in the ALU. A new control signal, ALUSel, selects the ALU operation: add or subtract. The control sets ALUSel from bit 30.

75
00:14:16,468 --> 00:14:26,047
The other R-format instructions work the same way. All of them have the same opcode. The fields funct7 and funct3 tell them apart.

76
00:14:26,497 --> 00:14:35,926
So the datapath does not change. The control decodes funct3 and funct7, and sets ALUSel to the right ALU function.

77
00:14:36,376 --> 00:14:58,861
The third instruction is addi. Here is addi x15, x1, minus 50. The immediate is in bits 31 to 20. rd gets rs1 plus the immediate. So ALU input B must now take the immediate. But for add, the same input takes register rs2. One wire cannot carry both values.

78
00:14:59,311 --> 00:15:19,514
Now we trace addi x15, x1, minus 50, with x1 equal to 100. The bar at the top keeps the instruction and the given values. Phase 1, fetch, is the same for every instruction. IMEM gives the instruction at 1000, and the adder gives 1004.

79
00:15:19,964 --> 00:15:29,692
Phase 2, decode and register read. The rs1 field holds 1. The register file outputs x1, which is 100.

80
00:15:30,142 --> 00:15:47,785
In parallel, the new immediate generator gets the immediate field, bits 31 to 20. In the bar, they are 1 1 1 1 1 1 0 0 1 1 1 0. ImmSel is I, so it outputs the 32-bit value minus 50.

81
00:15:48,235 --> 00:16:01,013
Phase 3, execute. ALU input B must get the immediate, not a register. So the new BSel mux sits in front of input B. BSel is 1, so it passes minus 50.

82
00:16:01,463 --> 00:16:09,165
DataA goes to ALU input A. The ALU adds 100 and minus 50. The result is 50.

83
00:16:09,615 --> 00:16:20,836
addi uses no memory. Phase 5, write back. 50 goes to DataD. The rd field holds 15, so AddrD is 15. RegWEn is 1.

84
00:16:21,286 --> 00:16:27,473
At the rising edge, x15 becomes 50, and the PC becomes 1004.

85
00:16:27,923 --> 00:16:46,440
Here is the whole addi datapath, as the slides draw it, with the values of our example. The new hardware is orange: the immediate generator and the BSel mux. The other I-format arithmetic instructions use the same path. Only ALUSel changes.

86
00:16:46,890 --> 00:16:56,063
How does the immediate generator build the I immediate? Start with the instruction bits of addi x15, x1, minus 50.

87
00:16:56,513 --> 00:17:04,172
The high 12 bits of the instruction, bits 31 to 20, are copied to the low 12 bits of the immediate.

88
00:17:04,622 --> 00:17:15,779
The upper 20 bits of the immediate are copies of instruction bit 31. This is sign extension. Here bit 31 is 1, so the upper bits are all 1.

89
00:17:16,229 --> 00:17:23,376
The result is hex F F F F F F C E. That is minus 50, the same value as the 12-bit field.

90
00:17:23,826 --> 00:17:43,197
Why copy bit 31? In two's complement, the top bit is the sign. For lw x14, 8 of x2, bit 31 is 0, so the upper bits are all 0, and the immediate is 8. A negative field stays negative, and a positive field stays positive.

91
00:17:43,647 --> 00:18:03,337
The fourth instruction is load word. Here is lw x14, 8 of x2, in the I-format. rd gets the memory word at address rs1 plus the immediate. So DataD must now take the memory data. But for add and addi, DataD takes the ALU result.

92
00:18:03,787 --> 00:18:20,491
Now we trace lw x14, 8 of x2. The bar gives x2 equal to 2000, and memory at 2008 holds 42. Phase 1, fetch. IMEM gives the instruction, and the adder gives 1004.

93
00:18:20,941 --> 00:18:32,355
Phase 2, decode and register read. The rs1 field holds 2. The register file outputs x2, which is 2000. This is the base address.

94
00:18:32,805 --> 00:18:43,727
The immediate generator takes the immediate field, bits 31 to 20. In the bar it is 8. ImmSel is I, so it outputs 8. This is the offset.

95
00:18:44,177 --> 00:18:58,428
Phase 3, execute. This is the same path as addi. BSel passes the immediate, and the ALU adds. 2000 plus 8 is 2008. For a load, this result is a memory address.

96
00:18:58,878 --> 00:19:07,966
Phase 4, memory. The new data memory, DMEM, gets the address 2008 from the ALU. MemRW is Read.

97
00:19:08,416 --> 00:19:18,912
DMEM outputs the word at address 2008 on DataR. The bar gives this word: 42. A memory read is also combinational.

98
00:19:19,362 --> 00:19:36,471
Phase 5, write back. DataD now has two possible sources: the memory data, or the ALU result for add and addi. So the new WBSel mux chooses. WBSel is 0, so it passes the memory data, 42.

99
00:19:36,921 --> 00:19:42,063
The rd field holds 14, so AddrD is 14. RegWEn is 1.

100
00:19:42,513 --> 00:19:54,246
At the rising edge, x14 becomes 42, and the PC becomes 1004. lw is the only instruction here that uses all five phases.

101
00:19:54,696 --> 00:20:13,939
Here is the whole datapath for lw, as the slides draw it. The values of our example stay on it. Gray parts exist, but lw does not use them. The new hardware for lw is orange: DMEM, the WBSel mux, MemRW, WBSel.

102
00:20:14,389 --> 00:20:29,877
All loads have the same opcode. funct3 gives the size and the signedness. Byte and halfword loads need extra circuits. They take the correct byte or halfword, and extend it to 32 bits before the write back.

103
00:20:30,327 --> 00:20:47,863
The fifth instruction is store word. Here is sw x14, 8 of x2. Memory at rs1 plus the immediate gets register rs2. A store has two source registers, so rs2 must stay at bits 24 to 20.

104
00:20:48,313 --> 00:20:58,595
So the S-format splits the offset. Bits 11 to 5 are at the top. Bits 4 to 0 are where rd would be. Together, the offset is 8.

105
00:20:59,045 --> 00:21:14,960
Now we trace sw x14, 8 of x2. The bar gives x2 equal to 2000, and x14 equal to 42. Phase 1, fetch. IMEM gives the instruction, and the adder gives 1004.

106
00:21:15,410 --> 00:21:25,991
Phase 2, decode and register read. The rs1 field holds 2. DataA outputs x2, which is 2000. This is the base address.

107
00:21:26,441 --> 00:21:39,241
A store reads a second register. The rs2 field, bits 24 to 20, holds 14. DataB outputs x14, which is 42. This is the data to store.

108
00:21:39,691 --> 00:21:56,395
The immediate generator now needs the S format. ImmSel is S, so it joins the two immediate fields in the bar: 0 0 0 0 0 0 0, then 0 1 0 0 0. The offset is 8.

109
00:21:56,845 --> 00:22:07,555
Phase 3, execute. BSel passes the immediate, and the ALU computes the address, as for a load. 2000 plus 8 is 2008.

110
00:22:08,005 --> 00:22:14,511
Phase 4, memory. The address 2008 goes to DMEM. MemRW is Write.

111
00:22:14,961 --> 00:22:24,881
DMEM also needs the data. The new part is one wire, from DataB to DataW, the memory data input. It carries 42.

112
00:22:25,331 --> 00:22:36,723
A store must not change a register, so RegWEn is 0, and phase 5 does nothing. The WBSel value has no effect. The star means don't care.

113
00:22:37,173 --> 00:22:49,120
At the rising edge, memory at 2008 becomes 42, and the PC becomes 1004. Like a register write, the memory write happens at the edge.

114
00:22:49,570 --> 00:23:04,546
Here is the whole sw datapath, as the slides draw it, with the values of our example. The new parts are orange: the wire from DataB to DataW, and the S format in the immediate generator.

115
00:23:04,996 --> 00:23:11,353
Now the immediate generator must handle two formats. Compare the I-format and the S-format.

116
00:23:11,803 --> 00:23:23,515
Immediate bits 11 to 5 are at the top in both. But bits 4 to 0 are in two places: bits 24 to 20 in I, and bits 11 to 7 in S.

117
00:23:23,965 --> 00:23:34,227
In both outputs, bit 31 fills the upper bits, and bits 30 to 25 go to immediate bits 10 to 5. These are fixed wires.

118
00:23:34,677 --> 00:23:45,023
Only the low five bits have two sources. A 5-bit mux selects one. ImmSel drives it: I for addi and lw, S for sw.

119
00:23:45,473 --> 00:24:12,609
The sixth instruction is beq, branch if equal. If register rs1 equals register rs2, the next PC is PC plus the B immediate. Otherwise, the next PC is PC plus 4. The bar shows our example: beq x1, x2, 16 at PC 1000. If it is taken, the next PC is 1016. If not, it is 1004. Three things are missing.

120
00:24:13,059 --> 00:24:24,899
Need 1. Someone must compare the two registers. The ALU will be busy, so we add a branch comparator. Its output BrEq is 1 when the two values are equal.

121
00:24:25,349 --> 00:24:42,395
Need 2. Someone must compute the target, PC plus the immediate. The slides reuse the ALU. But ALU input A carries register rs1 for the other instructions. Two values want one input, so we add the ASel mux.

122
00:24:42,845 --> 00:24:53,575
Need 3. The PC input now has two possible values: PC plus 4, or the target. So we add the PCSel mux in front of the PC.

123
00:24:54,025 --> 00:25:09,449
Now we trace beq x1, x2, 16 at PC 1000. The bar gives x1 and x2: both are 5. Phase 1, fetch. IMEM gives the instruction. The adder computes 1004.

124
00:25:09,899 --> 00:25:22,678
Phase 2, decode and register read. The rs1 field holds 1, and the rs2 field holds 2. The register file reads x1 and x2. Both are 5.

125
00:25:23,128 --> 00:25:32,237
ImmSel is B, so the immediate generator builds the branch offset from the two immediate fields in the bar. The offset is 16.

126
00:25:32,687 --> 00:25:44,015
Phase 3, execute. The new branch comparator gets both register values. 5 equals 5, so BrEq is 1. BrEq goes to the control.

127
00:25:44,465 --> 00:25:57,479
At the same time, the ALU computes the target. ALU input A must get the PC, so the new ASel mux sits in front of it. ASel is 1, so it passes the PC, 1000.

128
00:25:57,929 --> 00:26:06,697
BSel passes the immediate, 16. The ALU adds 1000 and 16. The target is 1016.

129
00:26:07,147 --> 00:26:28,352
The new PCSel mux chooses the next PC: PC plus 4 on input 0, or the ALU result on input 1. BrEq is 1, so the branch is taken, and PCSel passes 1016. A branch uses no memory and writes no register, so RegWEn is 0.

130
00:26:28,802 --> 00:26:35,991
At the rising edge, the PC becomes 1016. The next instruction comes from the target.

131
00:26:36,441 --> 00:26:54,916
Now let x2 be 7. The bar shows the new value. BrEq is 0, so the branch is not taken. PCSel passes PC plus 4, and the PC becomes 1004. The ALU still computes 1016, but the mux ignores it.

132
00:26:55,366 --> 00:27:08,102
Here is the whole beq datapath, as the slides draw it, with the taken case of our example. The new hardware is orange: the branch comparator, the ASel mux, and the PCSel mux.

133
00:27:08,552 --> 00:27:23,635
The branch comparator compares A and B. BrEq is 1 if A equals B. BrLT is 1 if A is less than B. BrUn equal to 1 selects an unsigned comparison for BrLT.

134
00:27:24,085 --> 00:27:38,037
These two outputs serve all six branches. beq is taken when BrEq is 1, bne when BrEq is 0. blt and bltu are taken when BrLT is 1.

135
00:27:38,487 --> 00:27:43,969
bge is taken when A is not less than B. bne is the opposite of beq.

136
00:27:44,419 --> 00:27:56,323
Branch offsets are multiples of 2 bytes, so offset bit 0 is always 0. The instruction does not store it. The B-format holds offset bits 12 to 1.

137
00:27:56,773 --> 00:28:00,208
Here is the S immediate again, for comparison.

138
00:28:00,658 --> 00:28:16,807
RISC-V keeps immediate bits 10 to 1 in the same place as in S, and wires a zero into bit 0. Instruction bit 7, which was bit 0 in S, becomes bit 11 in B. The sign comes from bit 31.

139
00:28:17,257 --> 00:28:27,647
So only bit 7 changes its role between S and B. The range is minus 4096 to plus 4094 bytes.

140
00:28:28,097 --> 00:28:58,177
The seventh instruction is jalr, jump and link register. The register rd gets PC plus 4, and the PC gets register rs1 plus the I immediate, with bit 0 cleared. In video 4, a function returns with jalr x0, 0 of x1. It jumps to the return address in x1. The bar shows our example: jalr x1, 4 of x5 at PC 1000, with x5 equal to 3000.

141
00:28:58,627 --> 00:29:16,163
Need 1. jalr writes PC plus 4 into rd. This is the return address. But DataD already takes the memory data or the ALU result. Now three values want one input, so WBSel gets a third input, number 2.

142
00:29:16,613 --> 00:29:34,639
Need 2. The next PC is register rs1 plus the immediate. The ALU can compute it, as for a load. ASel passes the register, BSel passes the immediate, and PCSel passes the ALU result. No new mux is necessary.

143
00:29:35,089 --> 00:29:43,537
Need 3. The target must have bit 0 equal to 0. An AND unit clears bit 0. The slides do not draw it.

144
00:29:43,987 --> 00:30:04,318
Now we trace jalr x1, 4 of x5 at PC 1000. The bar gives x5 equal to 3000. Phase 1, fetch. IMEM gives the instruction. The adder gives 1004. This time we need 1004 twice: it is also the return address.

145
00:30:04,768 --> 00:30:14,624
Phase 2, decode and register read. The rs1 field holds 5. The register file outputs x5, which is 3000.

146
00:30:15,074 --> 00:30:20,749
ImmSel is I, so the immediate generator turns the immediate field into 4.

147
00:30:21,199 --> 00:30:34,255
Phase 3, execute. ASel passes the register, and BSel the immediate. The ALU computes 3004. The AND unit clears bit 0, but bit 0 is already 0.

148
00:30:34,705 --> 00:30:40,017
PCSel passes the ALU result, 3004, toward the PC.

149
00:30:40,467 --> 00:30:59,923
jalr uses no memory. Phase 5, write back. The new wire takes PC plus 4 to input 2 of the WBSel mux. WBSel is 2, so 1004 goes to DataD. The rd field holds 1, so AddrD is 1, and RegWEn is 1.

150
00:31:00,373 --> 00:31:13,599
At the rising edge, x1 becomes 1004, and the PC becomes 3004. Later, jalr x0, 0 of x1 can jump back to 1004.

151
00:31:14,049 --> 00:31:32,225
Here is the whole datapath for jalr, as the slides draw it. The values of our example stay on it. Gray parts exist, but jalr does not use them. The new hardware for jalr is orange: the wire from PC + 4 to WBSel input 2.

152
00:31:32,675 --> 00:31:51,385
jal needs no new paths. rd gets PC plus 4, and the PC gets PC plus the J immediate. ASel selects the PC as in beq. PCSel and WBSel are as in jalr. Only the immediate generator needs the J format.

153
00:31:51,835 --> 00:32:01,136
With PC 1000 and offset 200, the next PC is 1200, and x1 gets 1004.

154
00:32:01,586 --> 00:32:23,474
Here is a summary of all the instructions. For each one: the ALU inputs, what the ALU output means, the data memory, the value that rd gets, and the next PC. For add and sub, the ALU gets two registers. Its output is the result, and it goes to rd. The next PC is PC plus 4.

155
00:32:23,924 --> 00:32:27,359
addi is the same, but input B is the I immediate.

156
00:32:27,809 --> 00:32:38,262
lw has the same ALU inputs as addi. But its output is an address. DMEM reads at that address, and rd gets the memory data.

157
00:32:38,712 --> 00:32:47,373
sw computes an address too, with the S immediate. DMEM writes register rs2. No register changes.

158
00:32:47,823 --> 00:33:00,133
beq gives the PC and the B immediate to the ALU. The output is the branch target. The next PC is the target if BrEq is 1, or else PC plus 4.

159
00:33:00,583 --> 00:33:13,084
jalr and jal compute a jump target. rd gets PC plus 4, and the next PC is the ALU output. jalr starts from a register. jal starts from the PC.

160
00:33:13,534 --> 00:33:23,219
So the ALU does three jobs: it computes a result, an address, or a target. The muxes choose its inputs, and where its output goes.

161
00:33:23,669 --> 00:33:44,917
Worked problem 1. The processor runs lw x5, minus 4 of x6, at PC 400. x6 is 3000, and memory at 2996 holds 7. Which parts does it use, which values flow, and what changes at the edge? Pause the video and try it.

162
00:33:45,367 --> 00:33:55,330
The question stays in the bar at the top. Fetch. The PC is 400. IMEM gives the instruction, and the adder gives 404.

163
00:33:55,780 --> 00:34:10,671
Decode. The rs1 field holds 6, so the register file reads x6, which is 3000. The immediate field is ten ones, then 0 0, so the immediate generator outputs minus 4.

164
00:34:11,121 --> 00:34:20,550
Execute. ASel selects the register and BSel the immediate. The ALU outputs 2996, the address.

165
00:34:21,000 --> 00:34:28,893
Memory. MemRW is Read. DMEM reads address 2996, and outputs 7.

166
00:34:29,343 --> 00:34:37,471
Write back. WBSel selects the memory output, 7. The rd field holds 5. RegWEn is 1.

167
00:34:37,921 --> 00:34:46,135
PCSel is plus 4. At the rising edge, x5 becomes 7, and the PC becomes 404.

168
00:34:46,585 --> 00:34:58,147
Part a. Here is the full datapath. The gray parts are not used: DataB, the branch comparator, PCSel input 1, and WBSel inputs 1 and 2.

169
00:34:58,597 --> 00:35:18,501
Worked problem 2. beq x5, x6, minus 8 is at PC 1040. Find the ALU output, BrEq, PCSel and the next PC, with x6 equal to 3, and then with x6 equal to 4. x5 is 3. Pause the video and try it.

170
00:35:18,951 --> 00:35:32,903
The question stays in the bar. Fetch and decode, for case a. The register file reads x5 and x6. Both are 3. ImmSel is B, and the immediate generator outputs minus 8.

171
00:35:33,353 --> 00:35:45,513
The ALU always computes the target: 1040 minus 8 is 1032. In case a, both registers are 3, so BrEq is 1.

172
00:35:45,963 --> 00:35:55,755
So PCSel selects the ALU, and the next PC is 1032. RegWEn is 0. A branch writes no register.

173
00:35:56,205 --> 00:36:14,637
In case b, x6 is 4. BrEq is 0. PCSel selects PC plus 4, and the next PC is 1044. The ALU still computes 1032, but the mux ignores it. In both cases RegWEn is 0.

174
00:36:15,087 --> 00:36:35,034
Worked problem 3. jalr x1, 3 of x5 is at PC 2000, and x5 is 3000. Find the ALU output, the next PC, and the value that x1 gets. Which inputs do PCSel and WBSel pass? Pause the video and try it.

175
00:36:35,484 --> 00:36:44,231
The question stays in the bar at the top. Fetch. IMEM gives the instruction at 2000. The adder gives 2004.

176
00:36:44,681 --> 00:36:54,579
Decode. The rs1 field holds 5, so the register file reads x5, which is 3000. The immediate field gives 3.

177
00:36:55,029 --> 00:37:08,576
Execute. ASel passes the register, BSel the immediate. The ALU output is 3003. This number is odd. The AND unit clears bit 0, so the target is 3002.

178
00:37:09,026 --> 00:37:13,399
PCSel passes the target, 3002, to the PC.

179
00:37:13,849 --> 00:37:22,191
WBSel is 2, so DataD gets PC plus 4, which is 2004. The rd field holds 1.

180
00:37:22,641 --> 00:37:37,318
At the rising edge, x1 becomes 2004, and the PC becomes 3002, not 3003. PCSel passes the ALU, and WBSel passes PC plus 4.

181
00:37:37,768 --> 00:37:44,445
Worked problem 4. For each instruction, mark the parts whose output it needs. Pause and try it.

182
00:37:44,895 --> 00:37:55,733
add uses IMEM, the register read, the ALU and the register write. addi also uses the immediate generator. lw adds a memory read.

183
00:37:56,183 --> 00:38:05,121
sw writes memory but no register. beq uses the comparator and the ALU, but no memory and no register write.

184
00:38:05,571 --> 00:38:09,667
jalr writes PC plus 4. jal reads no register.

185
00:38:10,117 --> 00:38:17,669
Only lw uses all five structures in series. Video 7 shows that it sets the clock period.

186
00:38:18,119 --> 00:38:31,047
Worked problem 5. The word is hex F E 5 3 2 A 2 3. Which ImmSel does the control choose, and what does the immediate generator output? Pause the video and try it.

187
00:38:31,497 --> 00:38:38,217
The opcode, bits 6 to 0, is the store opcode. So the format is S, and ImmSel is S.

188
00:38:38,667 --> 00:38:50,529
Immediate bits 11 to 5 come from bits 31 to 25: seven ones. Bits 4 to 0 come from bits 11 to 7: 1 0 1 0 0.

189
00:38:50,979 --> 00:39:03,352
Bit 31 is 1, so sign extension fills the upper bits with ones. The result is minus 12. The instruction is sw x5, minus 12 of x6.

190
00:39:03,802 --> 00:39:16,325
Worked problem 6. The word is hex F E 2 0 8 C E 3, a branch, so ImmSel is B. What does the immediate generator output? Pause the video and try it.

191
00:39:16,775 --> 00:39:29,660
Label the bits. Bit 31 gives immediate bit 12. Bit 7 gives bit 11. Bits 30 to 25 give bits 10 to 5. Bits 11 to 8 give bits 4 to 1.

192
00:39:30,110 --> 00:39:36,254
Put them in order, and add a zero for bit 0. The 13-bit value is minus 8.

193
00:39:36,704 --> 00:39:45,579
Sign extend from bit 31. The output is minus 8, so the target is the PC minus 8, two instructions back.

194
00:39:46,029 --> 00:39:59,341
Worked problem 7. The immediate generator gets hex 0 0 2 0 8 0 E 3. What is its output with ImmSel equal to S, and with B? Pause the video and try it.

195
00:39:59,791 --> 00:40:10,692
Only instruction bit 7 is 1 in the immediate fields. With ImmSel equal to S, bit 7 goes to immediate bit 0. The output is 1.

196
00:40:11,142 --> 00:40:17,862
With ImmSel equal to B, bit 7 goes to bit 11. The output is 2048.

197
00:40:18,312 --> 00:40:26,461
Recap. One. One instruction per cycle. Reads are combinational. State changes only at the rising edge.

198
00:40:26,911 --> 00:40:31,647
Two. The phases are fetch, decode, execute, memory, and write back.

199
00:40:32,097 --> 00:40:37,644
Three. The register numbers sit at fixed bits, so they go straight to the register file.

200
00:40:38,094 --> 00:40:45,177
Four. When two values want one input, add a mux: BSel, WBSel, ASel or PCSel.

201
00:40:45,627 --> 00:40:50,341
Five. Each instruction adds a few parts, in the order of the slides.

202
00:40:50,791 --> 00:40:57,597
Six. The immediate takes its sign from bit 31. Only the low bits and bit 11 need a choice.

203
00:40:58,047 --> 00:41:10,377
Seven. The ALU computes a result, an address, or a target. Stores and branches write no register. In video 7, we build the control. That is the end of video 6.
